    </seriesStmt><sourceDesc><p>Born digital.</p></sourceDesc></fileDesc><revisionDesc><change who="http://syriaca.org/documentation/editors.xml#autogenerated" when="2026-07-28-05:00">CREATED: This bibl record was autogenerated from a Zotero record.</change><change who="https://www.zotero.org/cbss_admin" when="2022-11-18T14:34:41Z">Record created in Zotero.</change></revisionDesc></teiHeader><text><body><biblStruct type="journalArticle"><analytic><author><forename>Paul S.</forename><surname>Russell</surname></author><title level="a">The Image of the Infant Jesus in Ephrem the Syrian</title><idno type="URI">http://syriaca.org/cbss/3Q6MDSY4</idno><idno type="URI">https://www.zotero.org/groups/a_comprehensive_bibliography_on_syriac_studies/items/3Q6MDSY4</idno><idno type="URI">https://www.zotero.org/groups/4861694/items/3Q6MDSY4</idno></analytic><monogr><title level="j">Hugoye: Journal of Syriac Studies</title><imprint><date>2002</date></imprint><biblScope unit="vol">5:1</biblScope></monogr><note type="abstract">This paper examines passages in which St. Ephrem the Syrian makes use of the image of Jesus Christ as an infant child. It demonstrates that he uses this tool to support his full picture of the Incarnation, including both a stress on a fully divine Divinity and on a fully human humanity. This study also makes clear that Ephrem imagines that the experience of the Divine Word in being incarnate has close affinities with the common human experience we all share. This has an interesting effect on his picture of how the incarnation figures in the working out of human salvation. Ephrem's view of the Incarnation is shown to be imaginatively full, making use of the subjective as well as the objective elements in human life and nature.</note></biblStruct><bibl type="formatted" subtype="bibliography" resp="https://www.zotero.org/styles/chicago-note-bibliography-17th-edition">
  Russell, Paul S. “The Image of the Infant Jesus in Ephrem the Syrian.” <title level="m">Hugoye: Journal of Syriac Studies</title> 5:1 (2002).
